WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.580
ASP.NET Core includes antiforgery support for defending against CSRF and XSRF-style risks in browser-based workflows.

2
00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:14.920
Conceptually, the server generates protected values that are associated with the intended application interaction.

3
00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:21.260
Protected state-changing requests are expected to include the required token material when they reach the server.

4
00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:24.420
Antiforgery tokens are not identity by themselves.

5
00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:28.200
They are request-intent evidence for a protected workflow.

6
00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:33.820
The server validates whether the expected token is present and valid for the protected request.

7
00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:42.320
If validation fails, the request should not proceed as a normal state-changing operation, even if the browser also sent an authentication cookie.

8
00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:45.720
Antiforgery validation is separate from authentication.

9
00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:49.980
Authentication establishes the user's identity for the request.

10
00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:55.680
A token validation check helps confirm that the request followed the expected browser application path.

11
00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:57.460
Both checks can matter.

12
00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:03.320
A request can be authenticated and still fail antiforgery validation.

13
00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:06.800
Antiforgery validation is also separate from authorization.

14
00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:12.020
Authorization decides whether the identified user is allowed to perform the action on the target resource.

15
00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:15.480
Antiforgery validation helps confirm request intent.

16
00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:22.300
A request can pass authorization logic and still be rejected because it did not include the expected antiforgery signal.

17
00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:28.500
ASP.NET Core antiforgery relies on the ASP.NET Core Data Protection system.

18
00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:34.120
Data Protection handles protected values used by framework features such as antiforgery and cookies.

19
00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:37.500
That means key persistence and key availability matter.

20
00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:46.000
In multi-instance deployments, instances need compatible Data Protection keys so token generation and validation remain reliable across the deployment.

21
00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:49.160
Token failure handling should be practical and safe.

22
00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:59.980
A failed validation should produce a controlled response, useful protected logs, and enough operational context to troubleshoot configuration or client-flow problems.

23
00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:05.200
It should not reveal sensitive token details or internal implementation data to callers.
